      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1031,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself. This creates a slight negative charge on the oxygen side and a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself and dissolve other substances.\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure that is less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking, washing, and most biological processes.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air as vapor.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is crucial for transporting nutrients and waste in living organisms.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together very strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the human body temperature stable.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it. It is the primary component of all living cells (about 60% of the human body is water) and is essential for:\n*   Transporting nutrients and oxygen to cells.\n*   Removing waste.\n*   Lubricating joints and organs.\n*   Regulating body temperature through sweating and panting."
